{"authored" [{"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+1)^{2}=-(x-3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-1 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-4{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-8t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/assets/6cd9c6a97d450761c01a9adb723985acbf4e5dc91e9877d7bd46c95db66d8c30.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(3,1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-7$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ -4 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-2){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb67ee7103b14ebe343eb7adab4f94f0aed04e66bb41f728230e171115f90223" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-4t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-4t,\\,\\,y-1=-2{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-84467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/assets/75df54dd30da590814e89153396ac7cf8a713fc9a6e04d14786780147ba57cfe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(5,-1),$ and passes through the point $(17,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(5,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(17,11),$ we can substitute $x=17$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be234a9581989edfb42eeeeddcfd27456b5f8bd4c753acbf9d610dfe6e2572f8"} {"question" "q-84467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+1=3t,\\,y-5=6{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+5=6{t}^{2},\\,y-1=3t"] ["math" "x-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t"] ["math" "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t"] ["math" "x-5=6t,\\,y+1=3{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-3=-6t,\\,y-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-3}{6},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-3}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & =-\\frac{y-1}{3} \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{y-1}{3}) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be234a9581989edfb42eeeeddcfd27456b5f8bd4c753acbf9d610dfe6e2572f8"} {"question" "q-84855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+3)^{2}=12(y+1)"] ["math" "(x+3)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y+1)"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-1)"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}=-6(y-1)"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-84855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/assets/6cd9c6a97d450761c01a9adb723985acbf4e5dc91e9877d7bd46c95db66d8c30.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(3,1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-7$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ -4 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-2){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be234a9581989edfb42eeeeddcfd27456b5f8bd4c753acbf9d610dfe6e2572f8"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-4t"] ["math" "x+3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t"] ["math" "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t"] ["math" "x-3=-4t,\\,\\,y-1=-2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+1)^{2}=-(x-3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-1 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be234a9581989edfb42eeeeddcfd27456b5f8bd4c753acbf9d610dfe6e2572f8"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x-1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x-3=-4{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-8t"] ["math" "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-321592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-321592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-321592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be234a9581989edfb42eeeeddcfd27456b5f8bd4c753acbf9d610dfe6e2572f8"} {"question" "q-321592" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-321592" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/state.json" "file_sha256" "8d7da103a16ebc4223ed3f88c82d582c781e7651010a5a277198f51b80579870"} {"question" "q-321592" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "c